WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.000
I don't feel I inherited
an English department,

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:05.560
I feel someone handed me
a ticking time bomb

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:08.120
because they wanted a woman
holding it when it explodes.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.680
By now, you've probably
finished The Chair,

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:12.960
Sandra Oh's pin-sharp
comedy drama

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:14.840
detailing the embattled
daily life

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:16.560
of the first woman
appointed as Chair

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.600
of the English Department
at a top-tier university.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.040
And like all good students,

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:22.880
you've probably
got questions.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:23.800
Let's go!

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:27.000
Is The Chair
based on a true story?

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:29.040
It's not.
It's an original idea

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:32.000
created by Amanda Peet
and Annie Julia Wyman.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:34.200
The institution, too,
is fictional.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:35.640
Instead of being
a real place,

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:37.920
Pembroke University feels
like an amalgamation

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.680
of numerous elite
US learning institutions.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:44.120
Everyone in this looks
familiar, who's in it?

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.400
She doesn't need an intro,
but Sandra Oh

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.120
of Grey's Anatomy
and Killing Eve

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:49.720
stars as Ji-Yoon Kim,

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.680
the first female chair
at Pembroke University.

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:54.160
It's one of those
first dedicated

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:55.440
comedy series roles,

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:57.200
although both Killing Eve
and Grey's

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:58.880
obviously had their moments.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.120
Jay Duplass of Transparent
is Professor Bill Dobson,

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:03.760
a self-destructive widower

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.600
who becomes mired
in controversy.

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:07.480
Heil Hitler.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:10.680
And you'll recognise Emmy
Award-winner Holland Taylor,

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:13.320
who delights as the savage
Professor Joan Hambling,

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:14.400
from loads of stuff.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:16.880
Well, I hope they didn't
have to look at your fanny

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:18.080
while you did that.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:21.120
Including The Practice,
Legally Blonde

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:22.360
and The Truman Show.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.200
More recently, in 2020,

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:26.120
she appeared
as the lovable Stormy

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:27.760
in To All The Boys 2,

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:29.440
and was nominated
for an Emmy

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.200
for Ryan Murphy's Hollywood.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:33.240
Acting isn't magic.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:34.600
It's a job.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:37.080
Nana Mensah from
Queen of Glory and Bonding

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:38.440
is Professor Yaz McKay,

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:40.440
and Bob Balaban
from The Monuments Men

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:42.000
and basically
every Wes Anderson movie

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:43.360
since Moonrise Kingdom

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.760
is Professor Rentz,
the old-school academic

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.160
threatened by
the ascendant McKay.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.520
David Morse from The
Green Mile is Dean Larson.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:51.760
And the show
is partly stolen

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.560
by 7-year-old Everly
Carganilla of Yes Day,

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:55.880
who features as Ju Ju,

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:57.640
Ji-Yoon's wonderful,
complex,

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:00.040
slightly terrifying
adopted daughter.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.000
Do you have fur
on your vagina?

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:06.000
And David Duchovny
is played by, yes…

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:07.400
David Duchovny

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:08.760
of The X Files fame.

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:12.200
Wait, did David Duchovny
really go to Princeton?

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:14.120
Yeah! And, in fact,

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:15.760
loads of the stuff
said about Duchovny,

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:17.160
who plays himself
in the show,

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:18.840
is actually based on fact.

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:19.960
Interesting.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:21.320
He attended
Princeton University,

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:23.240
where he graduated in 1982

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:25.720
with an A.B.
in English Literature.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:27.120
And, yes,
that really is the title

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:29.200
of Duchovny's dissertation.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:31.200
"The Schizophrenic Critique
of Pure Reason

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:32.800
in Beckett's Early Novels".

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:34.400
I only wrote
the one chapter.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:36.760
The only difference is that
he wrote it at Princeton,

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:39.240
not Yale,
although he did go to Yale,

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:41.280
earning a Master of Arts
in English Literature

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:43.560
before starting work
on an unfinished PhD

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:44.960
on Magic and Technology

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:47.000
in Contemporary Fiction
and Poetry.

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:48.720
Yes, really.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:50.960
Oh, and the music stuff
is real too.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:53.520
In 2007, he put
his acting career on hold

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:55.960
to spend more time
as a singer-songwriter.

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:00.480
I'll have a mind of winter

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:02.960
Only song I've ever heard
quote Wallace Stevens.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:04.240
Fuck, I hope so.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.400
D.B. Weiss
and David Benioff?

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:08.560
Why do those names
sound familiar?

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.120
Come on! This is Weiss
and Benioff's first series

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:14.040
since Game of Thrones
ended in 2019,

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:16.560
although this time they're
producing not writing.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:19.480
Dirty John and A Lot Like
Love star Amanda Peet

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.240
takes the lead
as executive producer

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:22.600
and showrunner of The Chair.

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:24.000
And it's written by Peet,

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:25.720
cocreator Annie Julia Wyman,

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:27.000
Richard E. Robbins,

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:29.440
Jennifer Kim
and Andrea Troyer.

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:31.880
Where is The Chair set?

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:34.920
The Chair was filmed
in Pittsburgh in early 2021,

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:37.320
presumably in winter
looking at the scenery,

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:39.320
and much of the show
is set in the city's

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:40.600
Lawrenceville neighbourhood.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:42.800
The city has
three universities:

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:44.120
Carnegie Mellon University,

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:46.760
the University of Pittsburgh
and Duquesne.

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:47.960
But as we mentioned before,

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:50.200
Pembroke is
entirely fictional.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:52.600
What is Doljabi?

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:54.440
It's a long-standing
Korean tradition

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:56.360
to celebrate
a baby's first birthday

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:58.720
with a grand ceremony
called Doljanchi.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.120
This is the event Bill
ends up sort of gatecrashing

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.720
in Episode Five,
'The Last Bus in Town'.

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:06.280
The highlight
of the ceremony is Doljabi.

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:08.160
The child is placed
in front of a selection

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:10.600
of six to eight items then
encouraged to grab them.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.840
The items they pick
represent their future,

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:15.160
whether it's
a particular career, wealth

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:16.280
or lifestyle.

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:17.320
In The Chair,

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:18.640
Minji's options
are represented

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:20.960
by a stethoscope,
pencil, paintbrush,

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:23.920
money, tennis ball
and white string.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.600
But really the items
can be anything

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:27.560
that represents
a particular future,

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:29.200
from space rockets
for astronauts

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:30.920
to microphones
for performers.

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:32.600
This isn't authentic!

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:35.360
She's clearly tampering
with the process right now.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:36.760
Let her make her own choice.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:39.800
What is Día de los Muertos?

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:42.240
Día de los Muertos is
a Latin American holiday

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:44.200
with strong associations
to Mexico,

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:46.120
celebrated on
the 1st of November.

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:48.800
It honours the dead with
festivals and celebrations,

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:51.600
combining indigenous Aztec
ritual with Catholicism

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:54.160
brought to the region
by Spanish conquistadors.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:56.040
The idea is that the dead
would be insulted

00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:58.720
by mourning or sadness,
so Día de los Muertos

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:00.640
celebrates their lives
instead.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:01.920
As well as the celebrations,

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.640
the dead are celebrated with
something called ofrendas,

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:07.520
small personal altars
honouring one person.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:10.520
Ofrendas are decorated with
things like flowers, candles

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:12.760
and mementos of the person
being remembered,

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:15.000
which is exactly what Ju Ju
helps Bill to create

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:16.720
for his late wife.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:18.320
She's gonna find you.

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:21.400
What is tenure

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:24.120
and why is it so important
to Professor McKay?

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:26.560
This one will be pretty
obvious to US viewers

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:28.600
but perhaps less so
to those elsewhere.

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:31.240
Tenure is a permanent
academic appointment,

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:32.600
which can only be terminated

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:34.600
in exceptional
circumstances.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:36.800
As well as providing
security and influence,

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:39.120
it also affords a degree
of academic freedom.

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:40.880
Your university
isn't gonna get rid of you

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:43.360
for researching the wrong
thing, for instance.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:44.680
That's why it's so important

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:46.760
to Nana Mensah's
Professor Yaz MacKay.

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:49.200
It's a sign she's made it
as an academic.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:51.160
And although she's
an established and important

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:53.000
member of the teaching staff
at Pembroke,

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:55.840
she'd be in a much better
position if she was tenured.

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:56.960
Thank you so much

00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:58.760
for agreeing to chair
my tenure case.

00:05:58.840 --> 00:05:59.960
Ji-Yoon gave me the news.

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:01.120
Surely.

